recommend kg -- the loom is landed and the lane's booked lap is next: the source's own port rendezvous, which closes %485's named gap. The shape is worked out. The source binds ephemeral_port, reads its port back with getsockname, and publishes it to a rendezvous path given on the command line, written to `<path>.partial` and renamed so a reader never sees half a number; the asker reads that path under a bounded poll, and the file's existence proves the socket is bound, which the demo's 50ms nanosleep never could. With no path given the elder well-known port stands, so it accretes rather than breaks. That removes the machine-owned number from every fixture that currently takes tools/fixtures/a/amphora_vessel_port_lock.sh, and the reading nothing can take today becomes available: two demos at once on one pier, no lock, both green. Two things still want your word rather than another lap -- %493, whose fix is a capability word in another lane's runner, and both living pins, which are full enough that this round spent six trims fitting one paragraph onto the card.
obs THE NUMBER MOVED ON THE REBASE, AND THE STAMP HELD. Booked %497 from a fetch at 20260906.143706; the rebase before the send brought six upstream commits, one of them publishing %497 and %498 on one shelf from a peer, and two more taking the spine to %500. Published never moves and unshared does, so this row is %502 -- booked %497, renumbered to %501 on the first rebase and to %502 on the second, its stamp unchanged throughout, and every replace anchored to its own sentence rather than to the bare number, which is the fault this round exists to prevent.
obs AND MY OWN GUARD VERIFIED THE RENUMBER, which is the loop closing in one lap. reds_citation reads numbered_disagree=0 and shelf_disagree=0 on the renumbered tree, over the card, the recital and the shelf together -- a check the last two renumberings of this same row had no instrument for.
obs AND THE REBASE MISORDERED MY INDEX ROW WITHOUT A CONFLICT. Five files conflicted and were resolved by hand; session-logs/date/README-index-20260906.md was not one of them, because git merged four upstream rows in above mine cleanly. The result read 20260906.133554 above 20260906.144208 -- an older row over a newer one on a shelf whose whole contract is newest first. index_row_bound caught it on the hot re-verify at rows_misordered=1 and named both stamps. A clean auto-merge is where a shelf's ORDER goes wrong, since order is the one property a three-way merge of appended lines cannot see; the conflicts I resolved by hand were never the risk. It happened TWICE, once per rebase, and the second firing named the habit: prepending under the delimiter is right only while your row is the newest, and after a rebase it may not be. The row is placed by comparing stamps now rather than by position.
obs THE ROW TOOK THREE NUMBERS AND NEVER MOVED. Booked %497 after a fetch, renumbered %501 when the first rebase brought a peer's %497 and %498 on one shelf, renumbered %502 when the second brought a peer's %501 stamped nine minutes before mine. Published never moves and unshared does, so the stamp 20260906.143706 is what identified the row through all three, exactly as the derived spine promises -- and this round's own guard read numbered_disagree=0 after each hop, which the two renumberings before it had no instrument for.
recommend kg -- the loom is landed and the lane's booked lap is next: the source's own port rendezvous, which closes %485's named gap. The source binds ephemeral_port, reads its port back with getsockname, and publishes it to a rendezvous path given on the command line, written to `<path>.partial` and renamed so a reader never sees half a number; the asker reads that path under a bounded poll, and the file's existence proves the socket is bound, which the demo's 50ms nanosleep never could. With no path given the elder well-known port stands, so it accretes rather than breaks -- and the reading nothing can take today becomes available: two demos at once on one pier, no lock, both green. Two things want your word rather than another lap. %493 is still OPEN and not mine, so standing_equipment reads roster_broken here for a second lap; its fix is a new capability word in another lane's runner probe. And both living pins are full: the ledger had nine bytes when I went to book a red I had caused, and the card took eight trims to hold one paragraph.
